<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">6 April in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">7 events that happened on 6 April, from 1320 to 1930.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1320</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Scottish barons date the Declaration of Arbroath</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Scotland's nobles sent Pope John XXII a letter asserting the kingdom's independence from England and their right to depose any king who submitted to it. It is one of the most celebrated statements of medieval national identity.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1652</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Jan van Riebeeck arrives at the Cape of Good Hope</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Jan van Riebeeck reached Table Bay to set up a supply station for the Dutch East India Company. The outpost grew into Cape Town and began European settlement in southern Africa.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1782</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Rama I takes the throne and founds the Chakri dynasty</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The general Chao Phraya Chakri became king of Siam as Rama I and soon moved the capital across the river to Bangkok. His Chakri dynasty still reigns in Thailand today.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1896</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">First modern Olympic Games open in Athens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">King George I of Greece opened the first modern Olympics at the Panathenaic Stadium. Revived by Pierre de Coubertin, the Games drew some 240 athletes from 14 nations.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1909</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Peary and Henson claim to reach the North Pole</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Robert Peary, Matthew Henson and four Inuit companions reported reaching the North Pole. Doubts over Peary's navigation mean historians still debate how close they actually came.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1917</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">United States declares war on Germany</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Congress approved President Wilson's request for war, provoked by unrestricted submarine warfare and the Zimmermann Telegram. American troops and resources helped tip the balance on the Western Front.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1930</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Gandhi breaks the salt law at Dandi</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At the end of his 24-day march to the sea, Mohandas Gandhi picked up a lump of natural salt at Dandi, defying the British monopoly. The gesture launched a mass campaign of civil disobedience across India.</p></li></ol>
